        "problem": "Given that $P(A)=0.6,$ $P(A\\cap B)=0.25,$ and $P(A\\cup B)=0.95,$ find $P({B}^{\u2032}).$\n\n$P({B}^{\u2032})=$\n{{field-1}}",
        "worked_solution": "First, we calculate $P(B)$ using the addition law:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A\\cup B) & =P(A)+P(B)-P(A\\cap B) \\\\ 0.95 & =0.6+P(B)-0.25 \\\\ P(B) & =0.95+0.25-0.6 \\\\  & =0.6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({B}^{\u2032}) & =1-P(B) \\\\  & =1-0.6 \\\\  & =0.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
